--TEST--
`"7" is int` is `false` where `"7" as ?int` is `7`, and the two rows a finer type system answers differently from PHP — a `uint` is not an `int` and binary data is not a `string` — `rule:types/type-test`, `rule:types/uint`, `rule:types/bytes`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The hole `is` closes. `as` asks what a value can *become* and reads
// `rule:types/conversion`'s table to answer; `is` asks what it already
// holds and reads the tag. A numeric string is the pair that separates
// them: `string -> int` is a conversion row, so `"7" as ?int` is `7`, while
// a `string` is not an `int`, so `"7" is int` is `false`.

class Show {
    public static function yn(bool $b): string {
        if ($b) {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }

    // `as ?int` answers `null` exactly where `as int` throws, so one line
    // can carry both halves of the conversion's answer.
    public static function asInt(mixed $m): string {
        return (($m as ?int) ?? -1) as string;
    }
}

mixed $seven = "7";
echo "seven-is-int=", Show::yn($seven is int), "\n";
echo "seven-is-string=", Show::yn($seven is string), "\n";
echo "seven-as-int=", Show::asInt($seven), "\n";

// The two questions agree only where the value already holds the type. A
// fractional float and a word each convert to nothing, and neither is an
// `int` either, so a conversion that fails is not a second spelling of the
// test.
mixed $half = 2.5;
mixed $word = "abc";
mixed $one = 1;
echo "half-is-int=", Show::yn($half is int), " as=", Show::asInt($half), "\n";
echo "word-is-int=", Show::yn($word is int), " as=", Show::asInt($word), "\n";
echo "one-is-int=", Show::yn($one is int), " as=", Show::asInt($one), "\n";

// `rule:types/uint`: `int` and `uint` are separate tags, so a value from a
// `BIGINT UNSIGNED` column answers `is uint` and not `is int`, where PHP's
// `is_int()` was true for both. `as ?int` still bridges the two, which is
// why the divergence is the type system's and not this operator's — `is` is
// only the first spelling that makes it reachable by a mechanical rewrite.
uint $u = 7;
mixed $unsigned = $u;
echo "unsigned-is-uint=", Show::yn($unsigned is uint), "\n";
echo "unsigned-is-int=", Show::yn($unsigned is int), "\n";
echo "unsigned-as-int=", Show::asInt($unsigned), "\n";

// `rule:types/bytes`: the same split one type over. Binary data answers
// `is bytes` where PHP's `is_string()` was true for it.
bytes $b = "ab" as bytes;
mixed $octets = $b;
echo "octets-is-bytes=", Show::yn($octets is bytes), "\n";
echo "octets-is-string=", Show::yn($octets is string), "\n";
echo "octets-as-string=", ($octets as ?string) ?? "-", "\n";
--EXPECT--
seven-is-int=n
seven-is-string=y
seven-as-int=7
half-is-int=n as=-1
word-is-int=n as=-1
one-is-int=y as=1
unsigned-is-uint=y
unsigned-is-int=n
unsigned-as-int=7
octets-is-bytes=y
octets-is-string=n
octets-as-string=ab
